    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d289">

                <pb facs="0289" n="282" sameAs="frus:frus1937v03/pg282"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/9029: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Johnson</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Nanking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-07-28T10:00:00+08:00">July 28, 1937—10
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received July 28—6 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>332. 1. Donald, adviser to Chiang Kai-shek, told an officer of the Embassy last
                    evening, that the National Government had ordered Sung Che Yuan to resist the
                    Japanese and pointed to a news article published at Nanking morning of July 27th
                    stating that the Government had received a telegram from Sung reporting that he
                    had issued instructions to this effect to the 29th Army. The informant said that
                    “fighting” was now inevitable.<anchor n="96" xml:id="d289-f1"/></p>
                <p>2. Local news agencies last night received reports, said to be from reliable
                    official source, that the Central Government troops in South Hopei had been
                    ordered to proceed north to Changhsintien just south of Lukouchiao, but this has
                    not been confirmed.</p>
                <p>Sent to the Department, Peiping, Tokyo, Tientsin, Hankow. By mail to
                    Shanghai.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d289-f1" n="96" xml:id="d289-f1-content">
                <p>The Ambassador’s telegram No. 334, July 28, noon, added: “It Is probable that
                    Minister for Foreign Affairs had knowledge of this fact when he made statement
                    to British Ambassador and myself reported in my 330, July 27, 8 p.m.”
                    (793.94/9022)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
